     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**It is an average/baseline offer for a Google L6 PM, but it is not a compelling offer for you.**\n\n* **Compensation comparison:** Your run-rate at Meta is **~$526,000/yr** ($240k base + $36k bonus + $250k equity). Google\u2019s offer delivers **~$552,000 in Year 1** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $270k equity). That is only a **~5% increase**, which is effectively a lateral financial move.\n* **Base salary is a pay cut:** Google is offering you $235,000, which is below your current $240,000 base and sits in the lower half of their listed range ($183,000 \u2013 $271,000).\n* **Missing components:** There is **no sign-on bonus**, which is standard when poaching a senior IC from a direct competitor like Meta to offset transition risk.\n* **The \"refresher cliff\":** As the recruiter noted, after Year 4 your new-hire grant finishes and equity settles around $250,000/year, resetting your total compensation back to ~$532,000/year (barely above your current Meta compensation).\n\nSince you were not actively looking and are happy at Meta, **you should not accept this offer as-is, but you should negotiate rather than immediately walking away.** You have the strongest possible leverage: you are a passive candidate with strong performance at their closest peer competitor.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What to Ask For (and Exact Numbers)\n\nTop-of-band L6 PM offers at Google in the Bay Area can reach **$620,000 \u2013 $680,000+ in Year 1 total compensation** with competitive leverage. \n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Google Initial Offer | Your Counter-Ask |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **$260,000** |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **$52,000** (20% of new base) |\n| **Equity (4-Yr GSU)** | ~$250,000/yr | $1,080,000 ($270k/yr) | **$1,320,000** ($330k/yr) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **~$717,000 \u2013 $742,000** |\n\n#### Why these targets work:\n1. **Base ($260k):** The range max is $271k. Moving from Meta IC6 to Google L6 should not entail a base salary reduction.\n2. **Equity ($1.32M / 4 years):** This raises the initial equity floor to $330k/yr, providing a meaningful step up above your current $250k/yr vesting rate.\n3. **Sign-on ($75k\u2013$100k):** This bridges the gap for walking away from your unvested Meta trajectory and makes Year 1 clearly asymmetric in your favor.\n\n---\n\n### 3. How to Negotiate (Script & Talking Points)\n\nSet up a call with the recruiter and use the following positioning:\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m impressed by the scope of the role. However, looking at the numbers, this is essentially lateral to what I currently make at Meta.*\n>\n> *I wasn't looking to move, I'm very well-supported where I am, and my steady-state compensation is around $526,000 with strong refresh trajectory. A 5% bump with a lower base salary and no sign-on bonus does not justify the risk and friction of changing companies.*\n>\n> *For me to sign, we would need to be closer to:*\n> * *Base salary:* ***$260,000***\n> * *Equity:* ***$1,320,000*** *over 4 years*\n> * *Sign-on bonus:* ***$80,000***\n>\n> *If we can get the package to that level, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 4. Decision Rule\n\n* **If they meet or come close (e.g., $250k+ base, $1.25M+ equity, $50k+ sign-on):** Accept if you are excited about the product area, team, and scope at Google.\n* **If they refuse to move significantly (e.g., total Year 1 remains under $580k):** Politely decline and stay at Meta. At that point, the financial upside is negligible relative to your current momentum and equity vesting.",
